{
  "abstract": "We prove a matching-extension theorem for graph-polynomial coefficients on disjoint adjacent-twin cliques. Given a nonzero coefficient with consecutive exponents on each clique, a matching of nonedges can be absorbed at nonuniform list bounds determined by matching coverage. Every projected component must contain a cycle or a designated vertex with one additional list color; a fully covered good root is allowed. A local duplicated-exponent target fixes every intermediate indegree, and cycle reversals have cancelling factor and permutation signs. The resulting coefficient has magnitude $|c|2^z$. Uniform lists of the original maximum exponent plus one suffice for every matching. This extends the earlier twin-pair argument to arbitrary clique orders and branching projected components. A six-state identity supplies a coefficient of magnitude six for every even ring of cliques with pair overlaps. For seven-clique rings it yields overlap lists $(6,7)$ and private lists $4+s(v)$, with a one-color good-root allowance, for arbitrary private matchings of either parity. It also gives reductions at the exact nine-color critical budget and permits matching edges at overlap vertices. Separately, singleton-color recoloring of a nine-critical exterior forces three ring contacts per source vertex in any failure. Combined with biplanar density, this excludes every full-five asymmetric attachment to the known $38$-vertex, $193$-edge source, including matching contacts and private clique-partition contacts of order at most three. Exhaustive finite head checks and independent whole-graph coefficient extraction corroborate the proofs. No global occurrence theorem or ten-chromatic biplanar graph is obtained.",
